## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming data structures, heap-based algorithms, and amortized complexity analysis in a real-time processing context.

## Key Concepts
- **Two-heap streaming median:** A max-heap stores the lower half; a min-heap stores the upper half.
- **Rebalancing invariant:** Heap sizes differ by at most one, ensuring the median is accessible in O(1).
- **Time complexity:** O(log n) per element for insertion plus O(log n) for rebalancing.
- **Space complexity:** O(n), proportional to total elements seen.

## Code Example
```javascript
insert(x) {
  if (L.isEmpty() || x <= L.max()) {
    L.insert(x);
  } else {
    R.insert(x);
  }
  rebalance();
}

rebalance() {
  if (L.size() > R.size() + 1) {
    R.insert(L.extractMax());
  } else if (R.size() > L.size() + 1) {
    L.insert(R.extractMin());
  }
}

getMedian() {
  if (L.size() > R.size()) return L.max();
  if (R.size() > L.size()) return R.min();
  return (L.max() + R.min()) / 2;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical sliding-window counting and maintaining top-k with bounded memory.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window data structure
- Hashmap for counts
- Top-k extraction in bounded time
- Deterministic tie-breaking

## Code Example
````javascript
// Sketch
const W = 10000;
const windowQ = [];
const counts = new Map();
function applyBatch(batch){
  for (const x of batch){
    windowQ.push(x);
    counts.set(x, (counts.get(x) || 0) + 1);
    if (windowQ.length > W){
      const y = windowQ.shift();
      const ny = (counts.get(y) - 1);
      if (ny) counts.set(y, ny);
      else counts.delete(y);
    }
  }
  const top3 = [...counts.entries()].sort((a,b)=>b[1]-a[1]).slice(0,3).map(e=>e[0]);
  return top3;
}
````

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to a dynamic window size?
- What optimizations exist to avoid full re-scan on every batch?

